## Claim
Text: "Speaker Robin Vos lost a Republican-held seat in the 2018 election for the first time."
Claim author: Robyn Vining
Claim date: October 25, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Did Robin Vos run for re-election to the Wisconsin State Assembly in the 2018 election?
Answer: Yes, Robin Vos ran for re-election to the Wisconsin State Assembly in the 2018 election, as the article notes that "Republicans lost just one seat in the Assembly" that year while Vos remained in his position as speaker, having been re-elected.

Source URL: https://www.cbs58.com/news/vos-emerges-as-wisconsin-republican-leader-as-walker-gets-ready-to-step-down

### What was the outcome of the 2018 election for Wisconsin State Assembly District 63, the district represented by Robin Vos?
Answer: According to the source, the outcome of the 2018 election for Wisconsin State Assembly District 63 was that incumbent Speaker Robin Vos defeated Democrat Joel Jacobsen, retaining his seat.

Source URL: https://madison.com/robin-vos-timeline/article_ce58f640-24d2-5fdb-9c8f-ce3a3930ba88.html

### Was Robin Vos the Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly at the time of the 2018 election?
Answer: Yes, Robin Vos was the Speaker of the Wisconsin State Assembly at the time of the 2018 election, as he "has been speaker since 2013" and the article discusses his role during that period.

Source URL: https://www.fox6now.com/news/boss-vos-as-walker-exits-robin-vos-ready-to-become-most-powerful-republican-in-wisconsin

### Had Robin Vos ever lost a general election for the Wisconsin State Assembly prior to 2018?
Answer: No, Robin Vos had never lost a general election for the Wisconsin State Assembly prior to 2018; the timeline shows he won every election from 2004 onward, including defeating opponents in 2006, 2008, 2012, 2014, and 2016.

Source URL: https://madison.com/robin-vos-timeline/article_ce58f640-24d2-5fdb-9c8f-ce3a3930ba88.html

### Is it accurate to state that Robin Vos lost his own seat in the 2018 election?
Answer: No, it is not accurate. The search result explicitly states that in November 2018, Robin Vos "defeated Democrat Joel Jacobsen," meaning he won re-election and did not lose his seat.

Source URL: https://madison.com/robin-vos-timeline/article_ce58f640-24d2-5fdb-9c8f-ce3a3930ba88.html

### Was the seat held by Robin Vos in 2018 considered a Republican-held seat?
Answer: Yes, the seat held by Robin Vos in 2018 was considered a Republican-held seat, as Vos is a Republican who has represented his Assembly district since 2004 and has been a key figure in building and maintaining Republican majorities in the Wisconsin Assembly.

Source URL: https://www.fox6now.com/news/boss-vos-as-walker-exits-robin-vos-ready-to-become-most-powerful-republican-in-wisconsin

### Did any Republican-held seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly change party control in the 2018 election?
Answer: Yes, at least one Republican-held seat in the Wisconsin State Assembly changed party control in the 2018 election, as the Republican majority was reduced from 64 seats to 63 seats, meaning Democrats flipped one seat previously held by a Republican.

Source URL: https://ballotpedia.org/Wisconsin_State_Assembly_elections,_2018

### If the claim refers to a different Republican-held seat (not Robin Vos’s own), which specific seat was lost, and was Robin Vos involved in that loss?
Answer: The search result indicates that the only Republican-held seat lost in the 2018 Wisconsin State Assembly election was District 14, which flipped to the Democratic Party. There is no mention of Robin Vos's involvement in that loss.

Source URL: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Wisconsin_State_Assembly_election

## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that Robin Vos successfully won re-election in 2018, defeating his Democratic opponent, and has never lost a general election for the Wisconsin State Assembly. While Republicans did lose one seat overall in the Assembly that year, that loss was not Vos’s own seat, and there is no evidence attributing that loss to him personally. The claim explicitly states that "Speaker Robin Vos lost a Republican-held seat," implying he himself lost his seat, which is directly contradicted by the evidence.

